  "content": {
    "id": "p43-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "14",
    "prompt": "Show that $y=(p-3)x^2+6x-(p+3)$ will always have $x$-intercepts that are rational numbers, for all integer values of $p$ (except $p=3$).",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\Delta=4p^2$ is a perfect square. The roots are $x=1$ and $x=-\\dfrac{p+3}{p-3}$, both rational for integer $p\\ne3$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=6^2-4(p-3)(-(p+3))\\\\\\Delta&=36+4(p-3)(p+3)\\\\\\Delta&=36+4(p^2-9)\\\\\\Delta&=4p^2\\end{align*}$$\nFor integer $p$, $\\sqrt\\Delta=2|p|$ is an integer. By the quadratic formula,\n$$x=\\frac{-6\\pm2|p|}{2(p-3)}.$$\nThe numerator and denominator are integers, and the denominator is non-zero because $p\\ne3$. Thus both roots are rational; their values are $1$ and $-\\dfrac{p+3}{p-3}$.\nAt $p=3$, the $x^2$ term disappears and $y=6x-6$ is a straight line with $x$-intercept $1$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 58
  },
